---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const lang = 'en-us';
const post = {
  title: 'Christmas Lights in Sorrento and Salerno',
  subtitle: 'Luci d\'Artista and M\'illumino d\'Inverno, the two winter light festivals of the Gulf of Naples, with dates, locations and how to see both on one trip',
  category: 'GUIDES',
  date: 'October 21, 2026',
  readTime: '10 min',
  image: '/images/luci-artista-salerno-hero.webp',
  imageAlt: "Pink flamingo light installation at the Villa Comunale in Salerno during Luci d'Artista",
  path: '/en-us/blog/christmas-lights-sorrento-salerno/',
};

const metaTitle = 'Christmas Lights Sorrento and Salerno: Dates 2026/2027';
const metaDescription = 'Salerno\'s Luci d\'Artista and Sorrento\'s M\'illumino d\'Inverno explained: expected dates for the 2026/2027 season, where the installations are, late running transport, and how to combine the two in one trip.';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/en-us/' },
  { name: 'Blog', url: '/en-us/blog/' },
  { name: 'Christmas Lights in Sorrento and Salerno', url: '/en-us/blog/christmas-lights-sorrento-salerno/' },
];

const quickInfo = [
  { label: 'Salerno event', value: 'Luci d\'Artista, 21st edition' },
  { label: 'Expected dates', value: '14 November 2026 to 1 February 2027' },
  { label: 'Cost', value: 'Free, the whole city is the exhibition' },
  { label: 'Sorrento event', value: 'M\'illumino d\'Inverno, 18th edition' },
  { label: 'Expected dates', value: 'Late November to mid January' },
  { label: 'Switch on', value: 'Piazza Tasso, early December' },
  { label: 'Distance apart', value: 'About 1h30 by train via Naples' },
  { label: 'Best time to go', value: 'Weekday evenings, from 17:00' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'When do Salerno\'s Luci d\'Artista start in 2026?',
    a: 'The twentieth edition ran from 14 November 2025 to 1 February 2026, and the published calendar for the twenty-first edition points to 14 November 2026 to 1 February 2027. The comune confirms the switch on date in the autumn, so check the municipal site before booking travel around a specific evening.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Luci d\'Artista free?',
    a: 'Yes. The installations are in public streets, squares and parks, so walking the route costs nothing. The only exceptions are the enclosed installations, above all the one in the Villa Comunale, where access is managed with a queue and timed entry on busy evenings.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where exactly are the installations in Salerno?',
    a: 'The core route runs through the Villa Comunale on Via Roma, the cathedral area, Piazza della Libertà, Corso Vittorio Emanuele, the medieval Via dei Mercanti and the Lungomare Trieste. Further works are placed in outlying districts such as Pastena, which is why the local metro and bus services matter.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When are Sorrento\'s Christmas lights on?',
    a: 'M\'illumino d\'Inverno usually covers late November to the middle of January. The seventeenth edition ran from 30 November 2025 to 18 January 2026, with the Piazza Tasso tree and the street lights switched on during the evening of 6 December, accompanied by the town band. The eighteenth edition is expected on a similar calendar.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I see both in one day?',
    a: 'Technically yes, since they are about an hour and a half apart by train through Naples, but it makes for a long day with two evenings\' worth of sightseeing crammed into one. Better to base yourself in one town and give the other its own evening, returning late by train.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do the coastal ferries run in winter?',
    a: 'No. The ferry services along the Amalfi Coast and to the islands stop for the winter, so moving between Sorrento and Salerno in December means the Circumvesuviana to Naples and a train on from there. SITA buses run but on reduced winter timetables.',
  },
];

const sidebarLinks = [
  { label: 'Salerno', href: '/en-us/amalfi-coast/salerno/' },
  { label: 'Sorrento', href: '/en-us/sorrento-peninsula/sorrento/' },
  { label: 'The Amalfi Coast by Train', href: '/en-us/blog/amalfi-coast-by-train/' },
  { label: 'SITA Bus', href: '/en-us/guide/sita-bus/' },
  { label: 'Parking and ZTL', href: '/en-us/guide/parking-ztl/' },
];

const authorRole = 'Local Expert · Amalfi Coast';

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'The Amalfi Coast by Train',
    category: 'GUIDES',
    date: 'August 10, 2026',
    image: '/images/treno-costiera-hero.webp',
    imageAlt: 'The Amalfi Coast by train',
    path: '/en-us/blog/amalfi-coast-by-train/'
  },
  {
    title: 'Sorrento vs Positano: Where to Stay on the Amalfi Coast',
    category: 'GUIDES',
    date: 'June 15, 2026',
    image: '/images/sorrento-vs-positano-hero.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Sorrento and Positano compared',
    path: '/en-us/blog/sorrento-vs-positano/'
  }
];

const imageCredit = {
  author: 'Marco Ebreo / Wikimedia Commons',
  sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luci_D%27Artista_2.jpg',
  license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
  licenseUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/',
};

const keyTakeaways = [
  'These are two separate festivals with different characters: Salerno\'s Luci d\'Artista is a city wide light art exhibition, Sorrento\'s M\'illumino d\'Inverno is a two month programme of events around a decorated town.',
  'Salerno\'s twenty-first edition is expected to run from 14 November 2026 to 1 February 2027, mirroring the twentieth edition exactly, and it is free.',
  'Sorrento normally switches on in Piazza Tasso in the first days of December and keeps the programme going to the middle of January.',
  'December weekends in Salerno are genuinely crowded, with queues for the Villa Comunale installation and traffic on the approach roads. Weekday evenings are a different experience.',
  'Winter means no ferries. Sorrento to Salerno is the Circumvesuviana to Naples and then a train, about an hour and a half in total.',
];
---

<BlogPostLayout
  lang={lang}
  post={post}
  metaTitle={metaTitle}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  quickInfo={quickInfo}
  faq={faq}
  sidebarLinks={sidebarLinks}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  relatedArticles={relatedArticles}
  imageCredit={imageCredit}
  pageKey="christmas-lights-sorrento-salerno"
>

  <!-- Key Takeaways -->
  <div class="bg-slate-900 text-white p-8 rounded-[2rem]">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
      <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-med-gold flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg>
      <h2 class="font-serif text-lg font-bold text-med-gold uppercase tracking-[0.15em]">Key Takeaways</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="space-y-4">
      {keyTakeaways.map((point) => (
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-med-gold flex-shrink-0 mt-2"></span>
          <span class="text-white/80 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">{point}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <div>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-xl leading-relaxed mb-6 font-light">
      The Amalfi Coast empties out in November, and then two towns at either end of the bay switch the lights on and fill up again, with an almost entirely Italian crowd. <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/salerno/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Salerno</a> has turned its streets into an open air exhibition of light art every winter for two decades, and <a href="/en-us/sorrento-peninsula/sorrento/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Sorrento</a> runs a two month programme of concerts, nativity scenes and markets under its own decorations. They are forty kilometres apart as the crow flies and, in winter, an hour and a half apart in practice. Here is how both work.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Salerno -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Salerno: Luci d'Artista</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Luci d'Artista started in the 2006 season and has grown into the largest event of its kind in southern Italy, drawing hundreds of thousands of visitors each winter. The idea is not strings of bulbs over a shopping street: the installations are built pieces, often enormous, themed each year around myth, nature, fable or the night sky, and the city is laid out as a route you walk rather than a square you stand in.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The twentieth edition opened on <strong>14 November 2025</strong> at the Villa Comunale on Via Roma and ran to <strong>1 February 2026</strong>. The calendar published for the twenty-first edition points to <strong>14 November 2026 to 1 February 2027</strong>, which would repeat those dates exactly. Treat that as the working assumption and confirm it on the comune's own site before fixing a date, because the switch on has moved by a few days in the past.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">The route</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Villa Comunale, the cathedral quarter, Piazza della Libertà, Corso Vittorio Emanuele, Via dei Mercanti and the Lungomare Trieste, with further works in outlying districts. Allow a full evening and comfortable shoes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Getting around late</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">The city's metropolitan line runs late through the season, until roughly 23:00 on ordinary days and into the small hours on Saturdays and the eve of holidays, which is what makes park and ride on the edge of town workable.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">The Villa Comunale</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">The flagship installation is in the public gardens and is the one with the queue. On December weekends the wait can run to well over an hour, so go early in the evening or on a weekday.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Coaches and campers</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Salerno publishes specific rules each year for tourist coach and camper access and parking during the event. If you are arriving in either, check them in advance: enforcement is real and the fines are not small.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Sorrento -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Sorrento: M'illumino d'Inverno</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Sorrento's version is smaller, warmer and built around a programme rather than around objects. M'illumino d'Inverno covers roughly two months and packs in over a hundred events: concerts of classical, jazz and Neapolitan music, puppet theatre, nativity scenes in the churches of the old town, craft markets, exhibitions and a children's garden.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      The seventeenth edition ran from <strong>30 November 2025 to 18 January 2026</strong>. The tree and the street lights came on during the evening of <strong>6 December</strong> in Piazza Tasso, with the town's Caruso band playing, which is the moment locals treat as the real start of the season. The eighteenth edition is expected to follow the same shape: a late November opening, the switch on in the first days of December and a close in the middle of January.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-8">
      The venues are all walkable: Piazza Tasso, Corso Italia, the churches and squares of the historic centre, the Villa Comunale with its view over the bay, and the Teatro Tasso. Because the town is compact and mostly pedestrian, this is the easier of the two to do with small children or anyone who does not want to walk several kilometres in the cold.
    </p>

    <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-med-gold pl-6 my-8">
      <p class="text-slate-700 text-xl font-serif italic leading-relaxed">
        "Salerno is something you go and look at. Sorrento is somewhere you spend the evening."
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  <!-- Both in one trip -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Doing Both on One Trip</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      In summer this would be a boat ride. In winter the coastal ferries are laid up, so the two towns are connected through Naples, and the journey is more straightforward than the map suggests.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
      <table class="w-full text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-white">
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">Leg</th>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">How</th>
            <th class="text-left p-5 font-serif font-bold text-med-gold">Time</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="bg-white">
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Sorrento to Naples</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">Circumvesuviana, or the Campania Express where it runs in winter</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">About 1h</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Naples to Salerno</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">High speed train from Napoli Centrale, or a regional service</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">35 min to 1h15</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-5 font-bold">Salerno to the coast villages</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">SITA bus on reduced winter timetables, no ferries</td>
            <td class="p-5 text-slate-600">40 min to Amalfi</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mt-6">
      The practical plan is to base yourself in one of the two and treat the other as an evening out. Salerno is the better base if the lights are the point of the trip, since you can walk the whole route from a central hotel and the station is in the middle of town. Sorrento is the better base for everything else, because from there the rest of the peninsula and the coast road are still reachable in winter. Our guide to <a href="/en-us/blog/amalfi-coast-by-train/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">the Amalfi Coast by train</a> covers the connections in detail.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Local Tips -->
  <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-slate-100">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-6">
      <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-med-gold flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18h6"/><path d="M10 22h4"/><path d="M15.09 14c.18-.98.65-1.74 1.41-2.5A4.65 4.61 0 0 0 18 8 6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 1 .23 2.23 1.5 3.5A4.61 4.61 0 0 1 8.91 14"/></svg>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">Expert Tips</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Go mid week, and go early in the evening</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">The lights are on from dusk, which in December means from around 17:00. A Tuesday at six in the evening and a Saturday at nine are two different cities. If you only have a weekend, start the route at the far end and walk back towards the centre against the crowd.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Park outside and take the metro</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Driving into central Salerno on a December evening is a bad idea. Leave the car at one of the peripheral car parks served by the metropolitan line, which runs late during the season. The same logic applies in Sorrento, where the centre is largely closed to cars: check the <a href="/en-us/guide/parking-ztl/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">parking and ZTL rules</a> first.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Confirm the dates before you book flights</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Both events are announced by their comune a few weeks ahead and the opening date can slip. The dates here reflect the published calendar and the pattern of the last editions, which is reliable for planning a week but not for betting a specific evening on.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Build the nativity scenes into the trip</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">December on this coast is presepe season, and the churches of Sorrento's old town and the villages along the coast road put out elaborate ones. Combined with a half empty <a href="/en-us/amalfi-coast/amalfi/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Amalfi</a> and winter hotel rates, it makes a strong argument for coming out of season.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</BlogPostLayout>
